<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as UNTAC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Transitional Authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> was a peacekeeping and administrative mission set up under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-agreements">Paris Peace Agreements</a>, operating from March 1992 to September 1993 under Yasushi Akashi. With some 22,000 military and civilian personnel it was then the largest United Nations operation, supervising the administration, repatriating refugees and organising the election of May 1993, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> refused to disarm.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The United Nations Transitional Authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> (UNTAC) was formed after the Paris Peace Accords of October 1991 and established in February 1992 by Security Council Resolution 745, in agreement with the State of Cambodia, the de facto government. It was the first occasion on which the United Nations directly took responsibility for administering an independent state, rather than only monitoring it. Its aim was to restore peace and civil government in a country ruined by decades of civil war, to hold elections leading to a new constitution and to begin rehabilitation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mandate was wide: supervision or control of foreign affairs, defence, finance, public security and information, the disarming and demobilising of the fighting factions, the promotion of human rights, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a> of refugees and mine clearance. UNTAC had its own radio station and jail. Yasushi Akashi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> headed it, with Lieutenant-General John Sanderson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> as force commander, and personnel came from 46 countries. The operation cost more than 1.6 billion dollars.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> sabotaged the mission and refused to take part in the election, and its forces were never disarmed or demobilised. Critics argued that UNTAC disarmed the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s local militias more thoroughly than the Khmer Rouge, which gained territory. In May 1993 more than 4 million Cambodians, about 90 percent of eligible voters, voted. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-ranariddh">Norodom Ranariddh</a>'s FUNCINPEC won 45.5 percent, ahead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a>'s Cambodian People's Party. A constitution was promulgated on 24 September 1993, establishing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a>, with Ranariddh and Hun Sen as first and second prime ministers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>UNTAC lost 78 people. Two Japanese personnel were killed in April and May 1993, which led to calls in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s Diet for withdrawal, refused by Prime Minister Miyazawa. Sihanouk had reservations about the operation, and the number of sex workers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> rose from about 6,000 in 1991 to over 20,000 after UNTAC arrived, with a possible link to the spread of HIV. The coalition between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party">Cambodian People's Party</a> stayed tense and ended in FUNCINPEC's attempted coup of July 1997, which the Cambodian People's Party defeated. The effort to try senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> leaders led to an agreement ratified on 4 October 2004; the first trials took place in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Paris Peace Agreements of October 1991 set up a Supreme National Council chaired by Sihanouk and placed Cambodia under the <b>United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia</b>, UNTAC, which oversaw elections.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-cambodia">State of Cambodia</a></li></ul></section>
